# 1️⃣ Started With a Basic Go Application

The initial application was a simple Go web application using the standard `net/http` package.

The application was responsible for:

* Starting an HTTP server
* Serving web pages
* Handling HTTP requests
* Returning application content

Initially, the application could be run directly on a local machine:

```bash
go run main.go
```

However, this approach does not provide a consistent deployment environment or automated deployment workflow.

So I started adding DevOps practices around the application.

---

# 2️⃣ Containerized the Go Application With Docker

The first major step was to package the Go application into a Docker container.

Instead of requiring users to manually install Go and configure the runtime environment, the application and its runtime dependencies could be packaged into an image.

Example:

```dockerfile
FROM golang:1.21 AS builder

WORKDIR /app

COPY go.mod go.sum ./
RUN go mod download

COPY . .

RUN CGO_ENABLED=0 GOOS=linux go build -o app .

FROM gcr.io/distroless/static-debian12

WORKDIR /app

COPY --from=builder /app/app .

EXPOSE 8080

CMD ["/app/app"]
```

---

# 3️⃣ Implemented a Multi-Stage Docker Build

Instead of using the complete Go development image as the production runtime, I used a **multi-stage Docker build**.

### Stage 1 — Build

```text
golang:1.21
```

This stage contains everything required to compile the Go application.

### Stage 2 — Runtime

```text
distroless/static
```

Only the compiled application is copied into the final image.

```text
Go Source Code
      ↓
Go Builder Image
      ↓
Compile Application
      ↓
Go Binary
      ↓
Distroless Runtime
```

### Benefits

* Smaller production image
* Reduced attack surface
* No unnecessary build tools in runtime
* Cleaner production environment
* Faster image transfer

The resulting application image was around **50 MB** in the implementation.

---

# 4️⃣ Tested the Docker Image Locally

After creating the Dockerfile, I built the image:

```bash
docker build -t afsar-go-web-app:latest .
```

Then ran the container:

```bash
docker run -p 8080:8080 afsar-go-web-app:latest
```

The application could then be accessed through:

```text
http://localhost:8080
```

I also used Docker commands to inspect the image and container.

---

# 5️⃣ Pushed the Image to Docker Hub

After validating the image locally, I pushed it to Docker Hub.

```bash
docker login
```

Tagged the image:

```bash
docker tag afsar-go-web-app:latest afsarsohail/afsar-go-web-app:latest
```

Then pushed it:

```bash
docker push afsarsohail/afsar-go-web-app:latest
```

Docker Hub became the **container image registry** for the application.

The flow became:

```text
Source Code
     ↓
Docker Build
     ↓
Docker Image
     ↓
Docker Hub
```

---

# 6️⃣ Deployed the Application on Kubernetes

After containerization, I created Kubernetes manifests to deploy the application.

The application was deployed using a Kubernetes `Deployment`.

Example:

```yaml
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: go-web-app
spec:
  replicas: 2

  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: go-web-app

  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: go-web-app

    spec:
      containers:
        - name: go-web-app
          image: afsarsohail/afsar-go-web-app:latest
          ports:
            - containerPort: 8080
```

I configured **2 replicas** to provide basic application redundancy.

Instead of running only one Pod:

```text
Pod
```

the application runs as:

```text
        Deployment
             │
       ┌─────┴─────┐
       ▼           ▼
     Pod 1       Pod 2
```

If one Pod fails, Kubernetes can maintain the desired replica count.

---

# 7️⃣ Created a Kubernetes Service

Pods are ephemeral, so I created a Kubernetes `Service` to provide a stable network endpoint.

Example:

```yaml
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: go-web-service
spec:
  selector:
    app: go-web-app

  ports:
    - port: 80
      targetPort: 8080

  type: ClusterIP
```

The Service provides:

```text
Service Port: 80
       ↓
Pod Port: 8080
```

It also provides load balancing across the application Pods selected by the Service.

---

# 8️⃣ Added Kubernetes Ingress

To expose the application through HTTP routing, I configured an Ingress.

The traffic flow became:

```text
User
 ↓
Ingress
 ↓
Service
 ↓
Pod
```

The Ingress handles external HTTP/HTTPS routing and forwards requests to the Kubernetes Service.

This provides a cleaner architecture than exposing every application Pod directly.

---

# 9️⃣ Converted Kubernetes Manifests Into a Helm Chart

Managing multiple raw YAML files can become difficult as applications grow.

So I converted the Kubernetes manifests into a **Helm Chart**.

The structure became:

```text
go-web-app/
│
├── Chart.yaml
├── values.yaml
│
└── templates/
    ├── deployment.yaml
    ├── service.yaml
    ├── ingress.yaml
    └── ...
```

Instead of hard-coding configuration values, I moved configurable values into:

```text
values.yaml
```

For example:

```yaml
replicaCount: 2

image:
  repository: afsarsohail/afsar-go-web-app
  tag: latest

service:
  type: ClusterIP
  port: 80
```

This makes the application easier to deploy across different environments.

